":"ENSG00000151834","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.867386,"donor_log2fc":-0.154279,"donor_lfcse":0.167345,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00596469,"degas_log2fc":-0.213423,"degas_padj":9.20091e-08}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","module":"pink","kme":0.47161,"hub":true,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.531952,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","module":"blue","kme":0.204511,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.391996,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","module":"pink","kme":0.299847,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.655614,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","module":"turquoise","kme":0.151201,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.398013,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","module":"brown","kme":0.143211,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000151834","entrez_id":["2555"],"uniprot":["P47869"],"ncbi_summary":"GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the mammalian brain where it acts at GABA-A receptors, which are ligand-gated chloride channels. Chloride conductance of these channels can be modulated by agents such as benzodiazepines that bind to the GABA-A receptor. At least 16 distinct subunits of GABA-A receptors have been identified. Alternatively spliced transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been found for this gene. [provided by RefSeq, Nov 2013].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P47869","text":"Alpha subunit of the heteropentameric ligand-gated chloride channel gated by gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), a major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the brain (PubMed:10449790, PubMed:29961870, PubMed:31032849). GABA-gated chloride channels, also named GABA(A) receptors (GABAAR), consist of five subunits arranged around a central pore and contain GABA active binding site(s) located at the alpha and beta subunit interfaces (By similarity). When activated by GABA, GABAARs selectively allow the flow of chloride anions across the cell membrane down their electrochemical gradient (PubMed:10449790). Chloride influx into the postsynaptic neuron following GABAAR opening decreases the neuron ability to generate a new action potential, thereby reducing nerve transmission (By similarity). The alpha-2 subunit exhibits synaptogenic activity together with beta-2 and very little to no activity together with beta-3, the gamma-2 subunit being necessary but not sufficient to induce rapid synaptic contacts formation (By similarity)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}